<p>Prices and free-plan limits were checked against vendor pages on November 8, 2026. Billing modes differ. ClickUp and Slab publish annual rates for the figures shown above; Taskade and Anytype provide a monthly/yearly selector; Nuclino publishes a starting price on its solutions pages. Check each vendor's current calculator before comparing a team invoice. The Notion baseline is Free at US$0, Plus at US$10 per member/month and Business at US$20, with a separate Custom Agent credit price after the free trial.</p>
<h2 id="why-switch">Why teams look for a Notion alternative</h2>
<p>Notion combines documents, wikis, databases and projects, so many teams start there. The reasons to compare another tool usually appear after the first workspace has grown. A team may want stronger task controls, a smaller and simpler wiki, a local copy of every note, more specific security controls or a different way to budget AI use.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Project delivery:</strong> Notion can track tasks, but a project team may need time tracking, workload, sprint reports and more detailed task hierarchy.</li><li><strong>Knowledge publishing:</strong> a company that mainly needs approved internal articles may prefer a focused wiki with fewer database choices.</li><li><strong>Offline and ownership:</strong> users who want plain local files may prefer Obsidian. Anytype is another local-first option with a different sync model.</li><li><strong>Workspace complexity:</strong> a flexible tool can become hard to maintain if every team creates different databases and status rules.</li><li><strong>Budget:</strong> per-member plans, custom domains and Custom Agent credits should be compared with the work actually used.</li></ul>
<h2 id="clickup">1. ClickUp: best for task-first project teams</h2>
<p>ClickUp is a better choice when the main job is assigning and tracking work. Tasks, lists, folders and spaces give projects a clear structure. The product also has Docs, goals, dashboards, Gantt views, time tracking and sprint features. A team can keep a brief beside the task list and report on delivery without building every workflow from scratch.</p>
<p>ClickUp's Free Forever plan is a useful starting point. Its official pricing FAQ lists Unlimited at US$7 per user/month and Business at US$12 per user/month when billed yearly. The current pricing page also presents AI options separately, so check whether ClickUp Brain or another AI add-on is required. ClickUp is a strong pick for project managers, agencies and teams that need task ownership. It is less attractive if the main need is a calm, wiki-first writing space with little project administration.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> deadlines, owners, dependencies, dashboards and delivery reports matter more than a flexible document tree. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> most work is long-form reference material and the team does not need a full task system.</p>
<h2 id="confluence">2. Confluence: best for enterprise knowledge and Jira teams</h2>
<p>Confluence is built around spaces and pages for team knowledge. It fits organizations that already use Jira or other Atlassian products and want a shared place for project documentation, meeting notes, decisions and internal guidance. Atlassian also offers whiteboards, databases and AI features across its product set, but the exact feature set depends on plan and connected products.</p>
<p>Confluence has a Free plan for up to 10 users. The Atlassian pricing page shows Standard from about US$5.42 per user/month in its displayed calculator, but the final amount depends on team size and billing mode. Its value is strongest when a company already has Atlassian administration and project workflows. A solo writer may find the space and permission model heavier than needed.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> the wiki must connect to Jira projects, team spaces and organization controls. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> you mainly want a personal notebook or a lightweight database for flexible work.</p>
<h2 id="slab">3. Slab: best for a focused company wiki</h2>
<p>Slab focuses on team knowledge rather than trying to become every team's project database. It gives a company a place for posts, topics and shared reference material. Its search connects knowledge from Slab and supported external sources, which helps when information is spread across several work apps. The focused model can make it easier to explain where a policy or process belongs.</p>
<p>Slab's public pricing lists Free for up to 10 users, Startup at US$6.67 per user/month and Business at US$12.50 per user/month when billed annually. Check the current plan page for feature and user limits. Slab is a strong choice for a team that wants a maintained wiki. It is not a direct replacement for Notion's flexible databases, project views or custom internal tools.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> the main job is writing, finding and maintaining team knowledge. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> you need complex project records, task dependencies or many custom database views.</p>
<h2 id="nuclino">4. Nuclino: best for a lightweight team workspace</h2>
<p>Nuclino combines collaborative documents, a team wiki and simple project views. The interface is designed to stay light, and teams can connect pages, use canvases and arrange information in list, board, table or graph views. Its Free plan allows up to 50 items, up to three canvases and 2 GB of total storage. The vendor's solutions pages list advanced plans starting from US$6 per user/month.</p>
<p>Nuclino is a good fit when Notion feels too open-ended. A small team can create pages, link related notes and keep a simple knowledge base without building a large system of databases. Business adds features such as AI, audit logs, team insights, SSO and a custom publishing domain. The trade-off is fewer deep database and customization options than Notion.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> you want a quick team wiki with simple collaboration. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> your workflow depends on complex relations, rollups or highly customized project databases.</p>
<h2 id="obsidian">5. Obsidian: best for local Markdown knowledge</h2>
<p>Obsidian stores notes as Markdown files in a local vault. The core app is free, does not require an account and lets you keep files on your own device. Backlinks, graph view and community plugins help users connect notes and adapt the editor. This makes it useful for research, personal knowledge management and people who prefer files they can open with other tools.</p>
<p>Obsidian Sync is listed at US$4 per user/month when billed annually or US$5 monthly. Publish is US$8 per site/month annually or US$10 monthly. The optional commercial license is US$50 per user/year, but Obsidian says it is not required for commercial use. Sync adds encrypted syncing and shared vaults. Obsidian is not a ready-made team wiki or project manager, so collaboration and structured records need plugins or a separate system.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> local files, offline access and long-term ownership matter most. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> your team needs managed permissions, live databases and a shared workspace with little setup.</p>
<h2 id="anytype">6. Anytype: best for local-first private knowledge</h2>
<p>Anytype is a local-first workspace built around objects, relations and shared spaces. It is aimed at people who want more control over their information and a different model from a cloud-first wiki. The product supports personal knowledge, projects and collaboration, with a focus on private data and peer-to-peer sync. Check its current documentation for the exact behavior of shared spaces and network sync before using it for a team that needs central administration.</p>
<p>The public pricing page lists Free with 100 MB of remote storage, Plus at US$4/month, Pro at US$8/month and Ultra at US$16/month. Those prices are displayed per plan rather than as a standard per-seat team quote. Anytype also has a separate Business offering with admin and SSO features. It is worth comparing when privacy and local-first design matter more than a conventional cloud workspace.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> private knowledge and local-first data are priorities. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> you need a familiar SaaS admin model or a large set of standard business integrations.</p>
<h2 id="taskade">7. Taskade: best for AI agents and app workflows</h2>
<p>Taskade combines projects, AI agents, automations and AI app building. It can be a Notion alternative when a team wants documents and project information to feed agents or workflows. The product is broader than a wiki. Its value is in building small apps and connecting workspace data to AI actions, not in copying every Notion database view.</p>
<p>Taskade's pricing page shows a Free plan for one user, Starter at US$6/month with three users and 10,000 monthly AI credits, Pro at US$16/month with 10 users and 50,000 credits, and Business at US$40/month with unlimited users and 150,000 credits in the displayed view. The page includes a monthly/yearly selector, so confirm the billing mode before checkout. Read the <a href="/reviews/taskade-review/">Taskade review</a> and its <a href="/alternatives/taskade-alternatives/">Taskade alternatives guide</a> if AI app workflows are part of your shortlist.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it when:</strong> you want projects, agents and app workflows in one place. <strong>Skip it when:</strong> you mainly need a traditional company wiki or a clean personal notes app.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-choose">How to choose the right alternative</h2>
<p>Write down the three workflows you use most in Notion. For example, a company may use a wiki, a product roadmap and a task board. Decide which one causes the most friction. If the problem is task ownership, test ClickUp. If the problem is finding approved knowledge, test Slab or Confluence. If the problem is personal note ownership, test Obsidian or Anytype. If the problem is a simple team wiki, test Nuclino.</p>
<p>Then compare the real cost. Count billable seats, guests, storage, AI credits, custom domains and integrations. A free plan may have an item limit, a seat limit or a local-only model. A lower monthly price does not help if the team has to buy another product to replace the feature it loses. Use the same sample workflow in each tool and record the setup time, permission steps and reporting work.</p>
<h2 id="migration-from-notion">How to move from Notion without losing important work</h2>
<ol><li>List the pages and databases that people still use. Archive old projects before exporting.</li><li>Export a small workspace sample as Markdown, HTML and CSV. Keep attachments with the export.</li><li>Check nested pages, links, images, database properties and dates. Do not assume a CSV contains formulas or relations.</li><li>Import the sample into the new tool. Taskade documents a Markdown import path for Notion exports, while Obsidian can work directly with Markdown files.</li><li>Rebuild the parts that do not transfer as native objects: relations, rollups, views, automations, permissions, formulas and dashboards.</li><li>Give users a short period to review the new workspace. Keep the original Notion workspace available until the team signs off.</li><li>Choose one system as the source of truth for each type of record. Avoid updating the same task in two tools.</li></ol>
<p>Notion's export guide says workspace exports can include HTML, Markdown and CSV plus uploaded files, but private pages that the exporter cannot access and content excluded by teamspace settings may be missing. It also says an export cannot instantly recreate the original workspace. Treat migration as a controlled project, not a file upload.</p>
<h2 id="when-to-stay-with-notion">When to stay with Notion</h2>
<p>Do not switch only because another tool has a lower starting price or a longer feature list. If your team already finds its docs, policies and project records in Notion, the better first step may be to clean up the workspace. Assign owners to key pages, remove duplicate databases, agree on status names and review sharing. That work can solve the real problem without moving every record.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Stay with Notion</strong> when linked docs, wikis and databases are used every week and the team can maintain them.</li><li><strong>Add a specialist tool</strong> when one workflow needs deeper task tracking, local notes or a dedicated knowledge base, but the rest of the workspace still works.</li><li><strong>Plan a full move</strong> when the same limits affect several teams, access rules are difficult to manage or the cost of keeping Notion plus extra tools is higher than a supported replacement.</li></ul>
<p>Before making the decision, run one real project in the alternative. Test search, permissions, export, task ownership and reporting with the people who will use it. Keep the original Notion workspace available until the new system passes that check.</p>
